      "decisionIndex": 12
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "With only 2 lands on the battlefield, Ancient Stirrings for 1 mana would have been more efficient than Malevolent Rumble for 2 mana. Ancient Stirrings looks at 5 cards (vs 4 for Rumble) and can find colorless cards including lands like Urza's Saga. More importantly, casting Ancient Stirrings for {G} would leave the second Grove untapped, allowing the player to also cast Blade of the Bloodchief ({1}) in the same turn, developing the board further. With Malevolent Rumble costing {1}{G}, both Groves are tapped and nothing else can be played. While Rumble does create a Spawn token and puts cards in the graveyard, at this stage finding a third land and maintaining tempo with a second spell is more valuable.",
      "actionTaken": "Cast Malevolent Rumble for {1}{G}, using both available lands",
      "betterLine": "Cast Ancient Stirrings for {G} from one Grove, keeping the other Grove open to also cast Blade of the Bloodchief for {1} in the same turn, developing two plays instead of one",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshotIndex": 40,
      "player": "Gem3P Romance",
      "decisionIndex": 14
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "With only 2 lands (both Groves) and a hand full of colored-mana spells (Basking Broodscale {1}{G}, Writhing Chrysalis {2}{R}{G}, Ancient Stirrings {G}, Unholy Heat {R}), picking Urza's Saga over Commercial District is suboptimal. Urza's Saga only produces {C}, sacrifices after 3 turns, and doesn't help cast any of the colored spells in hand. Commercial District would provide a permanent colored source ({R} or {G}) plus surveil 1, enabling the deck to actually function. The non-chosen Commercial District goes to the graveyard, losing this colored source permanently.",
      "actionTaken": "Chose Urza's Saga from Malevolent Rumble, sending Commercial District to graveyard.",
